<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suddhodana</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Śuddhodana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suddhodana was a chief of the Shakya clan and the father of Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha. Early texts describe him as a leader of the Shakya assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapilavastu">Kapilavastu</a>, while later tradition made him a king. His wife Maya died soon after the Buddha's birth, and her sister Mahapajapati raised the child.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suddhodana.jpg" alt="Suddhodana" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nomu420 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suddhodna_seated_on_a_throne_Roundel_2_ivory_tusk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The name Śuddhodana means &quot;he who grows pure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>&quot;. His father was Sihahanu and his mother Kaccanā, and his earliest predecessor was named as King Maha Sammatha, or the first king of the Kalpa. His chief consort was Maha Maya, with whom he had Siddhartha Gautama, later called Shakyamuni, the Sage of the Shakyas. When Maya died shortly after the birth, her sister Mahapajapati Gotami was raised to chief consort. They had a second son, Nanda, and a daughter, Sundari Nanda, who both later became Buddhist monastics. At the age of 16 Siddhartha married his cousin Yasodharā, niece of Maha Maya and Mahapajapati.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Śuddhodana led the Shakya, who lived in an oligarchic republic with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapilavastu">Kapilavastu</a>. Later accounts of the Buddha's life often call him a king, but modern scholars dispute this and most recent scholarship rejects it. Many hold that the Shakya republic was ruled by a council of the warrior and ministerial class that chose its leader, the rājā, who did not rule autocratically, since questions were debated in the council and decided by consensus.</p><p>By the time of Siddhartha's birth the republic had become a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Kingdom of Kosala, and the rājā took office with the approval of Kosala's king. The earliest Buddhist texts do not describe the family as royal. Later texts may have misread the Pali word rājā, which can mean king, prince, ruler or governor.</p><p>According to legend, Śuddhodana tried hard to keep Siddhartha from becoming a śramaṇa, forbidding him to leave the palace and surrounding him with sensual pleasures. At 29 Siddhartha left home after his first journey outside, an episode known as the Great Renunciation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Śuddhodana grieved at his son's departure and long sought him. Seven years later, when word of his enlightenment arrived, he sent nine emissaries to invite him back, but they joined the sangha after hearing him preach. He then sent Siddhartha's friend Kaludayi, who also became a monk but kept his word and invited the Buddha home. The Buddha returned and preached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a> to his father. Four years later, hearing that his father was near death, he came again to the palace and preached to him, and Śuddhodana is said to have reached arhatship on his deathbed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Śuddhodana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Shakyas were ruled by an assembly of clan heads that met in a council hall, with an elected chief; the Buddha's father <b>Suddhodana</b> was one such leader, later remembered as a king.</blockquote><a href="/state/shakya-republic">Shakya republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kapilavastu, in what is now <a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a>. Died in Kapilavastu, in what is now <a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27.58,83.06" aria-label="Kapilavastu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Suddhodana"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4475 5191h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4475 5191h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=27.58,83.06">What was here? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
